  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY THROUGH SATURDAY/...\nIssued at 149 PM CDT Sun Oct 11 2026\n\nTuesday through Wednesday night\n\nThere is an increasing likelihood for showers and storms starting \nTuesday evening. Tuesday guidance has an upper level ridge over the \nMidwest with a closed upper level low over the Western Conus. \nSurface high pressure will be situated over the Southeast US. The \n250 mb jet streak will be rounding the top of the ridge Tuesday \nthrough Wednesday night and as pointed out in the SPC Day 3 outlook, \nthere is a shortwave moving east north of the Great Lakes Tuesday \nthrough Wednesday. All together synoptic scale lift and support is \nthere for storm development. There is also consistent WAA at 850 mb \nwith the low level jet. \n\nAt the surface, the Monday cold front looks to stall across the \nIA/MO border before slowly moving south again overnight into \nWednesday morning. Along this boundary, dewpoints will be in the \nupper 60s to low 70s. PWATS of almost 2 inches are present in both \nthe GFS and ECMWF across southeast IA, northwest MO, and west \ncentral IL. The REFS joint probs for CAPE/shear are 40-70% across \nthat same region Tuesday evening. All this puts the latest chances \nfor rain overnight Tuesday at 60-90% areawide. The highest likelihood \nfor isolated severe storms (20-30%) will be southeast IA and \nnortheast MO, which the SPC Day 3 Convective Outlook has in a \nMarginal risk.\n\nThursday through the weekend\n\nContinued high moisture across the area with another weak wave means \nthere is another bout of showers and storms during the day Thursday \nwith a 60-80% chance. The axis for thunderstorm potential will again \nbe south of I-80. Then a cold front is progged to sweep through the \narea Friday, bringing with it much cooler temperatures. This is also \nwhere the long-term guidance starts to diverge. Both the GFS and \nECMWF have a trough moving ESE from Canada Thursday into Friday, but \nthe ECMWF has the upper level low over Colorado pinch off and move \nwest. The GFS, on the other hand, has the same upper low move east \nand merge with the trough. The models disagree on how cold the 850mb \ntemps will be. The ECMWF has minimum 850mb temps of 2C by 12Z \nFriday, while the GFS has -5C by 15Z Friday. Either way, the latter \nhalf of the week and into the weekend will see much cooler temps \ncloser to seasonable values and, depending on which solution \nverifies, we may see our first frost potential Saturday and Sunday.",
